{:edb/db-before-t 1081 :edb/db-after-t 1082 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534394 50 #inst "2026-10-07T00:01:41.549Z" 13194139534394 true] [17592186129004 1100 "A particle moves with a constant velocity of $\\mathbf{u}=(5\\mathbf{i} +2\\mathbf{j}) \\,\\textrm{km/h}$. Find the speed of the particle." 13194139534394 false] [17592186129004 1100 "A particle moves with a constant velocity of $\\mathbf{u}=(5\\mathbf{i} +2\\mathbf{j}) \\,\\textrm{km/h}.$ Find the speed of the particle." 13194139534394 true] [17592186129004 1145 "The speed of a particle equals the magnitude of its velocity.\n\nTherefore, the speed of the particle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}u & &= & 5i + 2j\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{5^{2} + 2^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{25 + 4} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{29}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nkilometers per hour." 13194139534394 false] [17592186129004 1145 "The speed of a particle equals the magnitude of its velocity.\n\nTherefore, the speed of the particle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|u| & =|5i+2j| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{29}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nkilometers per hour." 13194139534394 true] [17592186129005 1100 "A particle moves with constant acceleration. When $t=0 \\, \\textrm{h}$, the velocity of the particle is $4\\mathbf{i} \\, \\textrm{km/h}$, and when $t=3\\, \\textrm{h}$, the velocity of the particle is $(7\\mathbf{i}+15\\mathbf{j}) \\, \\textrm{km/h}$. Calculate the acceleration of the particle." 13194139534394 false] [17592186129005 1100 "A particle moves with constant acceleration. When $t=0 \\, \\textrm{h},$ the velocity of the particle is $4\\mathbf{i}  \\, \\textrm{km/h},$ and when $t=3\\, \\textrm{h},$ the velocity of the particle is $(7\\mathbf{i}+15\\mathbf{j})  \\, \\textrm{km/h}.$ Calculate the acceleration of the particle." 13194139534394 true] [17592186129005 1145 "Recall that when a particle moves with constant acceleration, the velocity vector at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{a}t\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{u}$ is the initial velocity, and $\\mathbf{a}$ is the acceleration of the particle.\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = 4\\mathbf{i}, \\qquad \\mathbf{v} = 7\\mathbf{i}+15\\mathbf{j}, \\qquad t =3\n$$\n\ninto the equation above, we can solve for $\\mathbf{a}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv &= u + at \\\\\n7i + 15j &= 4i + a(3) \\\\\n3i + 15j &= 3a \\\\\na &= \\frac{1}{3}(3i + 15j) \\\\\na &= (i + 5j)km/h^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534394 false] [17592186129005 1145 "Recall that when a particle moves with constant acceleration, the velocity vector at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{a}t,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{u}$ is the initial velocity, and $\\mathbf{a}$ is the acceleration of the particle.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = 4\\mathbf{i}, \\qquad\n\\mathbf{v} = 7\\mathbf{i}+15\\mathbf{j}, \\qquad\nt =3\n$$\n\ninto the equation above, we can solve for $\\mathbf{a}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =u+at \\\\ 7i+15j & =4i+a(3) \\\\ 3i+15j & =3a \\\\ a & =\\frac{1}{3}(3i+15j) \\\\ a & =(i+5j)\\,{\\text{km/h}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534394 true] [17592186129006 1100 "The velocity of a particle is $(\\mathbf{i} -4\\mathbf{j}) \\, \\textrm{m/s}$ when $t=0\\,\\textrm{s}$. If the particle moves with a constant acceleration of $(2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}) \\, \\textrm{m/s}^2$, find the velocity of the particle when $t=5 \\, \\textrm{s}$." 13194139534394 false] [17592186129006 1100 "The velocity of a particle is $(\\mathbf{i} -4\\mathbf{j}) \\, \\textrm{m/s}$ when $t=0\\,\\textrm{s}.$ If the particle moves with a constant acceleration of $(2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}) \\, \\textrm{m/s}^2,$ find the velocity of the particle when $t=5 \\, \\textrm{s}.$" 13194139534394 true] [17592186129006 1145 "Recall that when a particle moves with constant acceleration, the velocity vector at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{a}t\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{u}$ is the initial velocity, and $\\mathbf{a}$ is the acceleration of the particle.\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf u = \\mathbf{i} -4\\mathbf{j}, \\qquad \\mathbf a = 2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nTherefore, after\n\n$$\nt = 5 \\, \\textrm{s}\n$$\n\nthe velocity of the particle will be\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv &= u + at \\\\\n&= (i - 4j) + (2i + j)(5) \\\\\n&= (i - 4j) + (10i + 5j) \\\\\n&= (11i + j)m/s\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534394 false] [17592186129006 1145 "Recall that when a particle moves with constant acceleration, the velocity vector at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{a}t,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{u}$ is the initial velocity, and $\\mathbf{a}$ is the acceleration of the particle.\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf u = \\mathbf{i} -4\\mathbf{j}, \\qquad \\mathbf a = 2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j} .\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $t = 5 \\, \\textrm{s},$ the velocity of the particle will be\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =u+at \\\\  & =(i-4j)+(2i+j)(5) \\\\  & =(i-4j)+(10i+5j) \\\\  & =(11i+j)\\,\\text{m/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534394 true] [17592186129007 1100 "A particle moves with a constant acceleration of $(\\mathbf{i} + 2\\mathbf{j}) \\, \\textrm{km/h}^2$. The velocity of the particle is $(-\\mathbf{i} - \\mathbf{j}) \\, \\textrm{km/h}$ when $t=0\\,\\textrm{h}$. Find the time when the velocity of the particle equals $(3\\mathbf{i}+7\\mathbf{j}) \\, \\textrm{km/h}$." 13194139534394 false] [17592186129007 1100 "A particle moves with a constant acceleration of $(\\mathbf{i} + 2\\mathbf{j}) \\, \\textrm{km/h}^2.$ The velocity of the particle is $(-\\mathbf{i} - \\mathbf{j}) \\, \\textrm{km/h}$ when $t=0\\,\\textrm{h}.$ Find the time when the velocity of the particle equals $(3\\mathbf{i}+7\\mathbf{j})  \\, \\textrm{km/h}.$" 13194139534394 true] [17592186129007 1145 "Recall that when a particle moves with constant acceleration, the velocity vector at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{a}t\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{u}$ is the initial velocity, and $\\mathbf{a}$ is the acceleration of the particle.\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} = \\mathbf{i} + 2\\mathbf{j}, \\qquad \\mathbf{u} = -\\mathbf{i} -\\mathbf{j}, \\qquad \\mathbf{v} = 3\\mathbf{i}+7\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\ninto the equation above, we can solve for $t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv &= u + at \\\\\n3i + 7j &= (-i - j) + (i + 2j)t \\\\\n4i + 8j &= (i + 2j)t \\\\\n4i + 8j &= ti + 2tj\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the $\\mathbf i$ and $\\mathbf j$ components in the last equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{4 = t; 8 = 2t\n$$" 13194139534394 false] [17592186129007 1145 "Recall that when a particle moves with constant acceleration, the velocity vector at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{a}t,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{u}$ is the initial velocity, and $\\mathbf{a}$ is the acceleration of the particle.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} = \\mathbf{i} + 2\\mathbf{j}, \\qquad\n\\mathbf{u} = -\\mathbf{i} -\\mathbf{j}, \\qquad\n\\mathbf{v} = 3\\mathbf{i}+7\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\ninto the equation above, we can solve for $t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =u+at \\\\ 3i+7j & =(-i-j)+(i+2j)t \\\\ 4i+8j & =(i+2j)t \\\\ 4i+8j & =ti+2tj\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the $\\mathbf i$ and $\\mathbf j$ components in the last equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4=t \\\\ 8=2t\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534394 true] [17592186129184 1020 #edb/ref 17592186286007 13194139534394 true] [17592186129184 1020 #edb/ref 17592186286014 13194139534394 true] [17592186129184 1020 #edb/ref 17592186286021 13194139534394 true] [17592186129184 1020 #edb/ref 17592186286028 13194139534394 true] [17592186129184 1020 #edb/ref 17592186286092 13194139534394 true] [17592186129185 1020 #edb/ref 17592186286056 13194139534394 true] [17592186129185 1020 #edb/ref 17592186286070 13194139534394 true] [17592186129185 1020 #edb/ref 17592186286081 13194139534394 true] [17592186129185 1020 #edb/ref 17592186286087 13194139534394 true] [17592186129186 1020 #edb/ref 17592186286035 13194139534394 true] [17592186129186 1020 #edb/ref 17592186286042 13194139534394 true] [17592186129186 1020 #edb/ref 17592186286049 13194139534394 true] [17592186129186 1020 #edb/ref 17592186286075 13194139534394 true] [17592186129187 1020 #edb/ref 17592186286063 13194139534394 true] [17592186129187 1020 #edb/ref 17592186286095 13194139534394 true] [17592186129187 1020 #edb/ref 17592186286099 13194139534394 true] [17592186129413 1017 #edb/ref 17592186126284 13194139534394 false] [17592186129413 1017 #edb/ref 17592186286001 13194139534394 true] [17592186129413 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534394 true] [17592186129413 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nThe initial velocity of a particle is $(5i - j)km/h$. Find the speed of the particle after $2$ hours if the particle moves with a constant acceleration of $(i + 3j)km/h^{2}$." 13194139534394 false] [17592186129413 1100 "The initial velocity of a particle is $(5i-j)\\,\\text{km/h}.$ Find the speed of the particle after $2$ hours if the particle moves with a constant acceleration of $(i+3j)\\,{\\text{km/h}}^{2}.$" 13194139534394 true] [17592186129413 1145 "Recall that when a particle moves with constant acceleration, the velocity vector at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nv=u+at,\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is the initial velocity, and $a$ is the acceleration of the particle.\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\nu=5i-j,\\,a=i+3j.\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $2$ hours, the velocity of the particle will be\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =u+at \\\\  & =(5i-j)+(i+3j)(2) \\\\  & =(5i-j)+(2i+6j) \\\\  & =(7i+5j)\\,\\text{km/h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the speed of the particle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|v| & =|7i+5j| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{7}^{2}+{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{49+25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{74}\\,\\text{km/h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534394 true] [17592186129414 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534394 true] [17592186129414 1100 "A particle moves with constant acceleration. When $t = 0s$, the velocity of the particle is $(-2i - 8j)m/s$, and when $t = 4s$, the velocity of the particle is $2im/s$. Calculate the acceleration of the particle." 13194139534394 false] [17592186129414 1100 "A particle moves with constant acceleration. When $t=0\\,\\text{s},$ the velocity of the particle is $(-2i-8j)\\,\\text{m/s},$ and when $t=4\\,\\text{s},$ the velocity of the particle is $2i\\,\\text{m/s}.$ Calculate the acceleration of the particle." 13194139534394 true] [17592186129414 1145 "Recall that when a particle moves with constant acceleration, the velocity vector at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nv=u+at,\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is the initial velocity, and $a$ is the acceleration of the particle.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\nu=-2i-8j,\\,v=2i,\\,t=4,\n$$\n\ninto the equation above, we can solve for $a,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =u+at \\\\ 2i & =(-2i-8j)+a(4) \\\\ 4i+8j & =4a \\\\ a & =\\frac{1}{4}(4i+8j) \\\\  & =(i+2j)\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534394 true] [17592186129416 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534394 true] [17592186129416 1100 "A particle moves with a constant acceleration of $(3i + 2j)m/s^{2}$. The velocity of the particle is $(-7i - 6j)m/s$ when $t = 0s$. Find the time when the velocity of the particle equals $2im/s$." 13194139534394 false] [17592186129416 1100 "A particle moves with a constant acceleration of $(3i+2j)\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ The velocity of the particle is $(-7i-6j)\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s}.$ Find the time when the velocity of the particle equals $2i\\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139534394 true] [17592186129416 1145 "Recall that when a particle moves with constant acceleration, the velocity vector at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nv=u+at,\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is the initial velocity, and $a$ is the acceleration of the particle.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\na=3i+2j,\\,u=-7i-6j,\\,v=2i\n$$\n\ninto the equation above, we can solve for $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =u+at \\\\ 2i & =(-7i-6j)+(3i+2j)t \\\\ 9i+6j & =(3i+2j)t \\\\ 9i+6j & =3ti+2tj\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the $i$ and $j$ components in the last equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}9=3t \\\\ 6=2t\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,t=3\\,\\text{s}.\n$$" 13194139534394 true] [17592186129417 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534394 true] [17592186129417 1100 "A particle moves with a constant acceleration of $(i - 2j)m/s^{2}$. The velocity of the particle is $(2i - 5j)m/s$ when $t = 0s$. Find the time when the velocity of the particle equals $(3i - j)m/s$." 13194139534394 false] [17592186129417 1100 "A particle moves with a constant acceleration of $(i-2j)\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ The velocity of the particle is $(2i-5j)\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s}.$ Find the time when the velocity of the particle equals $(3i-j)\\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139534394 true] [17592186129417 1145 "Recall that when a particle moves with constant acceleration, the velocity vector at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nv=u+at,\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is the initial velocity, and $a$ is the acceleration of the particle.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\na=i-2j,\\,u=2i-5j,\\,v=3i-j\n$$\n\ninto the equation above, we can attempt to solve for $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =u+at \\\\ 3i-j & =(2i-5j)+(i-2j)t \\\\ i+4j & =(i-2j)t \\\\ i+4j & =ti-2tj\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the $i$ and $j$ components in the last equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}1=t \\\\ 4=-2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich has no solution." 13194139534394 true] [17592186286001 1000 #edb/ref 17592186286002 13194139534394 true] [17592186286001 1000 #edb/ref 17592186286003 13194139534394 true] [17592186286001 1000 #edb/ref 17592186286004 13194139534394 true] [17592186286001 1000 #edb/ref 17592186286005 13194139534394 true] [17592186286001 1000 #edb/ref 17592186286006 13194139534394 true] [17592186286001 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534394 true] [17592186286001 1041 "selection" 131941395